/*******************************************************************************
	Jitting
*******************************************************************************/
The list is how the program is held, not how it runs. Interpreted: testOP walks
the list. Jitted: the jitter walks it once at compile time and emits straight-
line code; seq flattens, if becomes a branch, a loop a back-edge, break a jump.
First step, before any emitter: a recon of the new ops under jitter;, measured
against the rules for new rules. Today the new ops hand over to run time.
A split rule (decide below, build above) adds one label level; check it there.

/*******************************************************************************
	Looking into future of integrating claude into kant
*******************************************************************************/
Integrating Claude as a kant assistant (Tony / Clay discussion, 2026-11-04). NOT SCHEDULED --
waits on kant growing, including what it works on (forms, typesetting, drawing), not just how
it works. HPDL.
    Question: can an integrated Claude advise and assist a kant user -- e.g. modify an existing
    form to do what they want -- for a user clueless about programming, and for a good
    programmer new to kant?
    Answer: yes, reasonable to plan for and expect. Caveat: kant is in no model's training data,
    so an assistant knows kant only from what kant hands it at run time (grammar, docs, examples,
    refusals). The question is not "can AI learn kant" but "does kant explain itself well
    enough". That part is ours.
        - Clueless user: judges results by looking (forms suit this); cannot catch a wrong
          answer, so the assistant must check, and kant must make checking possible.
        - Programmer new to kant: the assistant mostly translates from what they know.
    Preparation (rough):
        1 Keep kant self-describing: grammar as data, a form readable as data.
        2 Refuse loud, name the patient: silent wrong answers are invisible to a user and an
          assistant can act on a named refusal. Curing silent wrongs is preparation.
        3 One spelling per idea (cf. "no cast operator, make it a number first").
        4 An assistant hands a user a change as a kant directives file (insertAt / replaceAt,
          the comment-minion shape), with a preview and an undo.
        5 A small worked-example corpus: form, change, result. Teaches more than a manual.
        6 The channel: the assistant is another party on the cross-instance channel (why
          listener is parked rather than patched).
    User docs: kept SEPARATE from DesignDocs (which stay engineering notes for Clod and Clay)
    and kant-accessible, perhaps tagged by audience. First audience target: the experienced
    programmer new to kant. Mostly the what, a little of the why -- what the typesetting
    documenter would print. Deserves a deep design session on how to shape them to be most
    effective. HPDL.
    Background assumption (Tony's forecast): Apple's on-device AI work is taking off; expect a
    local Claude (e.g. on iPhone) backed by a data center. Implications: a small local context
    favours terse references and examples; the local side does the checking (preview, fixture,
    refusal) while the remote side drafts; local / remote / two incant instances are all one
    pattern -- parties on a channel.

