/*******************************************************************************
	Design notion: a rule as an operator -- NOT RULED, NOT SCHEDULED
*******************************************************************************/
(Tony and Clay, 2026-11-09; recon SEQ 578, docs/ruleAsOperator.md.) Every
kantGrammar rule now ends in an instruct, an op and an operand list, and testOP
runs ifThen, whileLoop, seq and printList like any operator. So a control rule
is a mixfix operator: keywords, with operand slots between them. The notion: a
rule declares its op; its labelled terms, in order, are its operand slots; one
shared default action builds the instruct through makeInstruct and refuses by
the slot's name when a slot hands up nothing. EXPRESSIOn would narrow to infix.
What the recon measured: a rule's label already holds exactly its slots, in
term order, keywords left out, an absent optional term simply absent. A rule
cannot carry op= as an attribute today: any name=value on a rule becomes a term
and the rule stops matching. Regular rules (If with ELSEIf, WHILe, Do, STARt,
BLOCk) could lose their hand actions; FOr, ITERATe, BRANCh, the prints,
SHORTCUt and INVOKEr keep theirs. The hard part is a keyword form in operand
position (x = if a; 1 else 2), where the ; is ambiguous; today it builds
nothing, silently.
  ⚠ DATED 2026-11-09 (SEQ 579): no longer silent -- a keyword inside an expression
  now refuses by name on the new road and the next statement runs. Keyword forms
  as expressions stay unruled.

